      "medias": [
        {
          "src": "https://cdnb.artstation.com/p/assets/images/images/092/163/157/large/maxime-gerardin-still-2025-09-25-111115-3-1-1.webp?1758849680",
          "type": "img"
        },
        {
          "src": "https://cdna.artstation.com/p/assets/images/images/092/135/178/large/maxime-gerardin-viewport.webp?1758792852",
          "type": "img"
        }
      ],
      "videoThumbnail": "./static/assets/thumbnails/big-machine.mp4",
      "imgMiniThumbnail": "https://cdnb.artstation.com/p/assets/covers/images/092/135/675/smaller_square/maxime-gerardin-maxime-gerardin-still-2025-09-25-111115-3-1-1.jpg?1758793697",
      "tags": [
        "All Aspects"
      ],
      "main": true,
      "links": [],
      "description": "This short project originated from just playing around, cutting shapes from textures.com photos and turning them into sloppy 3d models. I ended up making the cylindrical thing and then planned to throw it away. After seeing some enthusiasm for this project from friends and fellow artists, I decided to give it 2 more days of work. So this project is between 2 and 3 days of work total. A lot of people ask me how I do my projects so fast, but the truthis I spend 90% of my time experimenting on small scenes, different aspects of 3d, not affraid of failing because they're not real projects, and that's usually how you can get easily creative ! Sometimes, I accidentally make something cool and just push it a bit further, but there is a sentence that says \"You do 80% of the work in 20% of the time, and 20% of the rest of the work in 80% of the time\". For me, this means this project received the first 80% of love, but I'm too lazy to finetune the remaining 20% on this side experiment project."
    },
